A||||7||Borgio||Are chassis dynos accurate?||||1||
Z||000000||Borgio||11-17-2001||05:39 PM||||I heard that they aren't if you have a significant amount of power. Because the wheels can't transfer that much power without slipping or something. I don't know its just what I heard. Is this accurate? What do you guys think?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Borgio||11-17-2001||07:47 PM||||If no one replies, I'll assume they are accurate.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||11-17-2001||08:01 PM||||You are talking about a dyno that measures at the vehicles wheels?||||reg||1
Z||000003||ITB 2M4||11-18-2001||01:13 AM||||When NASCAR first started toying with the idea of making the cars that raced in their series more equal, they took the top finishing cars of each brand, and put them on a chassis dyno, to see how much power each brand was making.  Seeing as a NASCAR stock car makes well over 750hp, I'd say just about anything you would put on a chassis dyno will be pretty accurate.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||11-18-2001||02:43 AM||||Yes, chassis dynos are accurate.  Obviously, cheap dynos won't be as accurate as ones used by auto manufacturing companies, but close enough.  There is no way any car you own has enough power to slip the wheels on the dyno.  The drums are knurled so that they are just as sticky, if not more, as the road.  I work with chassis dynos daily, and they eat tires, not because they slip, but because the bite so much.  We don't load the vehicle any more than it is loaded during a normal drive, just strap it into position.<P>Again, anything you'll ever be testing will be fine.  If you consider 800 hp where "significant power" starts, well, yes you will slip the tires a little....when the car first starts applying power (or turbos hit boost).  Just like on the road, massive amounts of power will cause the tires to break loose, so you have to be skilled with the throttle or strap that thing down TIGHT.<P>Just a side note; if you really can't keep the power down on the dyno, should you be measuring it?  I mean, after your tires are no good, what's the point of the extra power?  Dynos don't measure from zero mph, so you can get a running start.  Just like on the road, it doesn't matter how much power your engine has if you can't utilize it.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||donalson||11-18-2001||12:22 PM||||they are accurate to an extent.<P>dif brands will calibrate somewhat differnetly.<P>air temp and humidity will change accual power output, dyno the samw car in denver (1 mile above sea level) and then at sea level and things will be dif again.<P>but the last guy is right in the wheels slipping...unless your making 750+ hp at the wheels you aren't likely to slip.<P>sport compact car did a good issue on dyno tuning and such a few months ago...when i get my stuff unpacked i'll dig it out and copy it for ya'll me thinks<P>mark<P>------------------<BR>'86 mr2 hardtop<P>mid-engine lover||||reg||1
Z||000006||Borgio||11-18-2001||03:53 PM||||Thanks guys.||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||11-18-2001||09:38 PM||||They're generally accurate to +- 5%.  More like +-10-15% if you start to 'figure in drivetrain losses' on your own.<P>Don't sweat 10hp ;)  But yeah, they work well enough.<P>Don't apply full power untill 3rd gear :)<P>And as 1FST2M6 will tell you, GET GOOD QUALITY STRAPS AND USE LOTS! ;)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
